      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Put the chicken thighs in a large saucepan. Cover with 1.5 litres of cold water. Add the ginger, garlic and white parts of the spring onions. Bring to a boil, then reduce to a steady simmer. Cook for 30 minutes, skimming any foam that rises."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "While the broth simmers, prepare the chashu. Tie the pork shoulder tightly with string. Heat a splash of oil in a separate pan. Sear the pork on all sides until browned. Add the soy sauce, mirin and sake. Add 100ml of water. Cover and simmer on a very low heat for 25 minutes, turning occasionally."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Soft-boil the eggs. Lower them into boiling water and cook for exactly 7 minutes. Transfer immediately to cold water. Peel once cool."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Strain the chicken broth through a fine sieve. Return to the pan. Add the dashi stock. Taste. The broth should be clean and savoury but not salty yet. Keep warm over a low heat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the tare. Stir together 40ml soy sauce, 20ml mirin and 20ml sake in a small bowl."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook the noodles in a separate pan of boiling water following the packet instructions. Drain and divide between 4 warmed bowls."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add about 15ml of tare to each bowl. Ladle the hot broth over the noodles. Top with sliced chashu pork, a halved soft-boiled egg, spring onion greens and sesame seeds. Finish with a few drops of sesame oil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The broth looks cloudy instead of clear",
              "cause": "Broth boiled too vigorously or foam was not skimmed",
              "fix": "Always skim the foam in the first 5 minutes and keep the broth at a gentle simmer rather than a rolling boil. Strain through a fine sieve lined with muslin for a cleaner result."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The bowl tastes under-seasoned",
              "cause": "Not enough tare was added",
              "fix": "Add another teaspoon of tare to the bowl and taste again. The tare does the seasoning work so the broth stays clean."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The chashu is dry",
              "cause": "Cooked at too high a temperature or for too long",
              "fix": "Keep the heat very low during the braising step. The liquid should barely tremble. Rest the pork for 10 minutes before slicing."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Shio ramen is most closely associated with Hakodate in Hokkaido, where Chinese immigrants brought noodle soup to Japan in the late 19th century. The cold northern climate suited the warming clear broth. It is considered the oldest of the regional ramen styles. The restraint of the seasoning means the quality of the broth has nowhere to hide, making it a favourite benchmark for ramen cooks who want to test their stock-making skills."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
